Kernel semantic probes: second expansion
=======================================

61 declarations across 14 areas, in 17 kernel source files. The matching
22 BSDInstruments profiles live in cddl/usr.sbin/bsdinstruments/profiles;
the existing Makefile installs all .d profiles into the base catalog.
The desktop discovers that catalog; no separate hard-coded list is needed.
No system installation is performed by these source changes.

Disabled cost
-------------
On this tree's amd64 SDT implementation, a disabled call site is a patchable
nop instruction. Probe arguments are evaluated only in the enabled branch.
Without KDTRACE_HOOKS, the declarations and calls compile away. There is
still code/metadata footprint with hooks compiled in, and enabled probes
can incur substantial cost, especially stacks, printing, or actions under
locks. A predicate that rejects an event is not the same as disabling its
probe. No new normal-path timestamps, allocations or locks were added.
USB's alternate USB_GLOBAL_INCLUDE_FILE environment uses empty local macros.

Conventions
-----------
The JSON inventory provides source, declaration argument types, and probe
names. Arguments below are in arg0 order. C double underscores become
DTrace hyphens. Empty module/function fields should be wildcarded, e.g.
usb:::transfer-done. Pointer arguments are transient identities, not handles
that a later event can safely dereference. Names are copied by scripts at
the event. Kernel addresses may be reused; scope recording intervals to the
workload. No packet, audio, crypto, firmware or filesystem event payloads
are copied by probes.

The profiles aggregate until exit; durations are elapsed microseconds,
including sleep, not CPU time. Start before reproducing the issue. Missing
start events produce no latency sample. DTrace drops can invalidate timings;
check errors and drops before interpreting any capture. Use bounded recording
intervals for dynamic-variable storage and high-cardinality identities.
Filters apply to the executing context: PID filters on hardware or worker
callbacks do NOT select the originating application. Use device, queue or
operation identity when diagnosing asynchronous work. Completion clauses
are unfiltered so that starts selected in another context can be retired.
A profile requires its named providers; do not use -Z for live qualification
and treat silent/missing events as success. Offline checks use -Z only to
compile future probe names on a host without a DTrace device.

Contracts and limits
--------------------
inotify
  queue: instance, watch descriptor, event mask, rename cookie, pending count,
    outcome (0 enqueued, 1 record allocation shortage, 2 coalesced duplicate,
    3 queue limit). Loss probes precede overflow conversion; outcome 0 may
    report the resulting IN_Q_OVERFLOW record. Do not sum every outcome as
    dropped events. Pending count is post-insertion for 0, pre-insertion
    otherwise. Allocation shortage can recur while an overflow is queued.
  watch-remove: instance, watch identity, watch descriptor, before unlink.
  inotify-loss reports outcomes and queue depths, not userspace read delivery.

netlink
  request: PCB, sequence, protocol, message type after length validation.
  result: PCB, sequence, message type, handler/translation errno, bad-attribute
    offset. A result is not proof an ACK reached userspace; it is emitted
    even when ACK is suppressed or not requested. EINTR retains its meaning
    in the Netlink dispatcher. Sequence may be reused between requests.
  malformed: PCB, sequence, declared length, remaining length.
  rx-drop: PCB, message count, bytes in an outgoing batch rejected at the
    socket receive-buffer limit. Does not count all allocation/translation
    failures. Dumps are visible through their handler result, not individual
    NLMSG_DONE delivery qualification.
  netlink-errors times dispatch and counts statuses and dropped batches.

wlan
  state-request: VAP, old state, requested state, state-machine argument.
  state-result: VAP, old state, requested state, resulting state, return code.
    These bracket the main deferred state-machine callback; direct iv_newstate
    calls in coordination/reinit paths are not claimed as covered.
  assoc-fail: VAP, scan association failure reason (not errno).
  assoc-success: VAP. Neither event copies the peer address.
  scan-done: VAP, next channel index, final index, at the driver-requested
    scan completion path before the scanner callback. Not a general scan
    timeout signal: ieee80211_scan_timeout ages cached scan entries.
  wifi-state counts these transitions and outcomes.

usb
  transfer-start: transfer, USB device, endpoint address, when marked active.
  transfer-done: transfer, device, endpoint address, USB error, requested bytes,
    actual bytes after completion validation, before the client callback.
    Includes cancellation, timeouts and short-transfer status. USB errors
    are usb_error_t values, not errno. DMA-delay deferrals do not emit done.
  endpoint-stalled: transfer, endpoint identity when pipe start finds a stall.
    This is a stalled attempt, not a unique endpoint state transition.
  usb-latency measures submission through callback preparation, including
    queue/DMA delay, not solely hardware service time or callback execution.

pcm
  xrun: channel, direction (0 playback, 1 recording), hard-buffer ready bytes,
    wanted bytes (playback) / soft-buffer free bytes (recording), at the
    existing xrun detection branches. Detection is not exact glitch duration.
  trigger: channel, PCM trigger, driver return code for changed common triggers.
    No-op and non-common trigger paths are outside this probe's scope.
  audio-xruns reports detections, buffer distributions and trigger results.

netgraph
  queue: node, item, reader/writer mode, at insertion under queue lock.
  dispatch: node, item, NGQF type, nesting depth before handler dispatch.
  dispatch-done: node, item identity, error before dropping node/hook refs.
    Item may already have been freed; never dereference it here. A zero error
    can include a silent discard when no handler exists. Synchronous handler
    forwarding can nest; counts are per dispatch, not unique packets.
  disconnect: node, hook before invalidation under topology lock.
  netgraph-path counts routing/dispatch activity without printing payloads.

krpc
  dg-retry: client, XID, retransmit ordinal, retry interval in ticks before
    the next exponential backoff update.
  dg-done: client, XID, enum clnt_stat at common exit; early closed-client
    refusal is outside the scope. This is not an errno result.
  connect-start/done: client / client, enum clnt_stat for the serialized
    reconnect attempt, including TCP/RDMA setup. Early already-connected,
    closed, and interrupted-wait exits do not constitute an attempt.
  replay-hit: cache, XID, reply-present boolean; a hit can still fail when
    copying the saved reply. This is not proof of reply transmission.
  rpc-retries counts retry ordinals/statuses and measures connect attempts.

geom
  io-retry: bio, provider, bio command, ENOMEM at resource resubmission.
  io-complete: bio, provider, error, direct-delivery boolean before returning
    completion upstream. Consumer-less bios bypass this path. No identity
    is dereferenced after biodone. Counts include individual GEOM layers.
  geom-retries supplements the existing general geom-io/io-latency profiles.

busdma
  reserve-fail: DMA tag, map, pages needed for a NOWAIT reservation failure.
  defer: tag, map, pages needed when queued for bounce resources.
  ready: tag, map when resources are reserved and callback work is queued.
  callback: tag, map immediately before the deferred load is retried with
    the driver lock held. This is callback dispatch, not completion status.
  dma-waits separates resource wait from total time to callback dispatch.
  subr_busdma_bounce.c is included by architecture wrappers; compile the
    wrapper, not that file as an independent translation unit.

Grouped taskqueues (provider gtaskqueue)
  enqueue/coalesce: queue, task. reject: queue, task, EAGAIN.
  cancel: queue, task after removing any queued instance, before clearing
    TASK_ENQUEUED. A running task can remain active; this is not completion.
  execute-start/done: queue, task, queue execution sequence. Start is emitted
    under the queue lock before releasing it and entering an optional epoch;
    done follows the callback. Task may free itself; done uses identity only.
  drain-start/done: queue, task around the locked drain, also in grouptask_block.
  gtaskqueue-latency pairs execution by sequence and clears queue timing on
    cancellation. Sequence can wrap. taskqueue-latency uses the first batch's
    equivalent execution/drain probes (no queue-residence claim).

epoch
  wait-start/done: epoch, preemptible boolean after early-boot checks. These
    cover synchronization; preemptible timing also includes binding/priority
    restoration. Non-preemptible probes execute in a critical section.
  drain-start/done: epoch; includes serialization and both callback passes,
    excludes the final PICKUP_GIANT after drain. No per-reader probes added.
  epoch-stalls measures grace-period and drain duration, not blocker identity.

uma
  limit-wait: zone, zone name, maximum items immediately before sleepq_wait.
  limit-resume: zone after that sleep. A wake can be followed by another sleep.
  alloc-fail: zone, name, domain, allocation flags at direct-item allocation
    failures, including limit/import/init/constructor paths; not all bucket
    or fast-path failures. Constructor failures keep existing counter semantics.
  reclaim-start: zone, name, request, domain at uma_zone_reclaim_domain entry.
  reclaim-done: zone at return. Not every internal zone_reclaim call.
  uma-pressure supplements existing uma-alloc allocation/free activity.

firmware
  lookup: image name, initial registry-hit boolean in firmware_get_flags.
  module-start/done: image name / image name, linker errno. Successful linking
    does not imply the module registered the requested firmware.
  binary-start/done: image name / image name, registered-success boolean.
    Boolean result avoids inventing errno where existing paths have none.
  firmware-load copies image names for aggregation; no binary data is read.

ktls
  mode-change: socket, previous mode, new mode at committed TX replacement.
  mode-failed: socket, previous mode, requested mode, errno for backend setup,
    socket send-lock failure or replacement race after retaining a session.
    Early validation/disabled-offload refusals are not included.
  tx-fallback: socket, hardware setup error before attempting software TX.
  disable-done: socket, errno after the automatic retransmission-driven
    disable helper attempts software mode; early disconnected exit excluded.
  enqueue: first mbuf in batch, socket, page count on software TX submission.
  dispatch: first mbuf at worker dispatch (also pending sequential batches).
  crypto-done: mbuf, socket, error at asynchronous encryption completion only.
  drop: socket, error at ktls_drop entry. No key or plaintext access.
  ktls-fallback times batch queue residence; not per-record end-to-end crypto
    latency, and not receive-side offload coverage.

Profiles for the first expansion
-------------------------------
linux-futex-waits, linux-epoll-waits, rfcomm-stalls, ffs-pressure,
bufcache-waits, tty-flow, linux-socket-compat, and taskqueue-latency consume
contracts documented in KERNEL-PROBES. Native/compat Linuxulator providers
are matched together. Raw futex wait results, TTY notifications and FFS
cleanup pressure must not be interpreted as final syscall/device results.

Qualification
-------------
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/kernel-next-compile.py \
    --kernel-obj /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC-NODEBUG
  python3.12 tools/test/system-tracing/kernel-profile-check.py

The compiler matrix uses every logical CPU, hooks on/off, and prepared kernel
and module headers. The profile check renders both unfiltered and PID-filtered
scripts, compiles them offline, and checks selectors/argument bounds against
source inventories. These do not substitute for live workload tests.

Live gates: enumerate required probes without -Z; load relevant modules;
exercise success and failure paths for each area; verify counts against
functional results; validate positive timings and cancellation/reuse cleanup;
check DTrace ERROR and drop reports. USB/audio/wireless need real devices or
validated emulation. Pressure tests belong in a disposable VM. Verify the
booted kernel/modules match the source snapshot before claiming coverage.
